WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.130 --> 00:00:07.549
You guys, they did it, the rabbit has been
saved.

00:00:07.549 --> 00:00:10.660
Easy 10/10 move from Craig Mazin.

00:00:10.660 --> 00:00:13.550
So, was the episode good?

00:00:13.550 --> 00:00:15.500
Yeah, it was pretty great.

00:00:15.500 --> 00:00:19.570
We're kind of back to the first couple of
episodes in terms of adaption where very little

00:00:19.570 --> 00:00:21.570
was changed from the game to the show.

00:00:21.570 --> 00:00:26.340
And this comes with good reason because this
portion of the game was pretty amazing and

00:00:26.340 --> 00:00:28.550
translates really well for the show.

00:00:28.550 --> 00:00:32.480
Now obviously there are some action beats
that were removed from the game, like Ellie

00:00:32.480 --> 00:00:37.061
and David fighting off hordes of undead and
Joel basically killing the entire town, but

00:00:37.061 --> 00:00:40.070
I think what was retained was perfect for
the show.

00:00:40.070 --> 00:00:44.370
The amount of dramatic buildup was balanced
out really well with the action and some of

00:00:44.370 --> 00:00:48.329
the best scenes from the game made its way
into the show, like Joel interrogating the

00:00:48.329 --> 00:00:50.780
men for the location of the town.

00:00:50.780 --> 00:00:55.079
Joel is not messing around and seeing him
without hesitation meld right back into his

00:00:55.079 --> 00:00:59.440
more savage past self to find Ellie shows
how much he cares for her.

00:00:59.440 --> 00:01:03.629
That’s why him torturing these people in
this scene kind of excites you because it

00:01:03.629 --> 00:01:05.960
ignites his drive to find Ellie.

00:01:05.960 --> 00:01:10.729
Him doing these horrific acts ironically expresses
how much he loves Ellie.

00:01:10.729 --> 00:01:14.880
Which this essentially leads into my favorite
aspect of this particular story arc where,

00:01:14.880 --> 00:01:19.330
it’s set up that Joel is going to be the
savior, but Ellie is the one to overcome all

00:01:19.330 --> 00:01:20.750
of the conflict.

00:01:20.750 --> 00:01:25.000
It makes her character significantly more
important within the story because we see

00:01:25.000 --> 00:01:29.690
her succumb to extreme violent actions and
she is able to take care of herself.

00:01:29.690 --> 00:01:33.610
I adore the writing here from how clever it
was for Ellie to overcome David.

00:01:33.610 --> 00:01:37.680
Through quick thinking and being smart, the
thing that ironically saves her and buys her

00:01:37.680 --> 00:01:39.960
enough time is her bite wound.

00:01:39.960 --> 00:01:43.220
Throughout the show, she has been told not
to tell anyone about it, but the irony in

00:01:43.220 --> 00:01:46.969
that her telling these people about it saves
her is extra juicy.

00:01:46.969 --> 00:01:47.969
And R.I.P.

00:01:47.969 --> 00:01:48.969
video game Joel.

00:01:48.969 --> 00:01:53.250
The amount of respect coming from the creators
in terms of the source material and casting

00:01:53.250 --> 00:01:56.260
legacy actors from the games is really nice.

00:01:56.260 --> 00:02:01.420
But going back to the climax, In an outstanding
move by Neil Druckman, he simultaneously nails

00:02:01.420 --> 00:02:06.070
a big dramatic point for Ellie’s character,
while also defying set expectations within

00:02:06.070 --> 00:02:09.349
storytelling in which the main protagonist
saves the day.

00:02:09.349 --> 00:02:13.920
That’s why this segment of the story is
so genius because it works so well on both

00:02:13.920 --> 00:02:14.920
of these levels.

00:02:14.920 --> 00:02:19.281
We are led to believe that Joel will save
Ellie through him interrogating people, just

00:02:19.281 --> 00:02:22.819
for it to be more surprising when Ellie is
the one to kill David.

00:02:22.819 --> 00:02:26.970
Throughout this sequence by utilizing parallel
editing, the creators set up a dual perspective

00:02:26.970 --> 00:02:31.490
of Joel and Ellie, with the assumption that
Joel will intervene at a crucial point.

00:02:31.490 --> 00:02:37.099
However, that crucial point isn’t to save
her physically, and instead emotionally.

00:02:37.099 --> 00:02:42.420
She just brutally killed another human being
who was trying to rape her and she is utterly

00:02:42.420 --> 00:02:43.420
broken.

00:02:43.420 --> 00:02:47.500
The only person in the world to save her from
this mental space is her now surrogate father

00:02:47.500 --> 00:02:55.540
and the moment Joel calls her his baby girl,
it cements this emotional bond even more.

00:02:55.540 --> 00:03:00.599
This dramatic payoff and their long separation
coming to close here is immensely satisfying

00:03:00.599 --> 00:03:03.480
and is even more powerful due to what Ellie
just did.

00:03:03.480 --> 00:03:08.010
Bella Ramsey over the past two episodes on
their own puts her into the legendary status

00:03:08.010 --> 00:03:09.290
for actors.

00:03:09.290 --> 00:03:14.959
She has masterfully portrayed the crazy lows
and highs of the character and every emotional

00:03:14.959 --> 00:03:16.690
beat is done without fault.

00:03:16.690 --> 00:03:21.400
I came into this show expecting to be blown
away by Pedro Pascal as Joel, but I ended

00:03:21.400 --> 00:03:25.010
up getting floored by Bella Ramsey’s performance
even more.

00:03:25.010 --> 00:03:28.879
Which doesn’t mean that Pedro is doing a
bad job by any means, the acting is just on

00:03:28.879 --> 00:03:33.040
a completely different level overall in comparison
to other shows.

00:03:33.040 --> 00:03:38.230
Looking more specifically at David, I think
his adaption from the game is also perfect.

00:03:38.230 --> 00:03:42.660
I love the slow progression throughout the
episode as we learn different reasons as to

00:03:42.660 --> 00:03:44.799
why this guy is so messed up.

00:03:44.799 --> 00:03:49.629
Because it starts out with him seemingly being
a good guy, but as we peel away the layers

00:03:49.629 --> 00:03:51.890
he becomes more and more like the devil.

00:03:51.890 --> 00:03:57.760
He’s a manipulative, unfair, cruel, disgusting,
and vile person, and seeing each one of these

00:03:57.760 --> 00:04:00.860
traits reveal themselves was very fun throughout
the episode.

00:04:00.860 --> 00:04:06.069
He’s a very engaging character because as
a viewer we need to see past this facade that

00:04:06.069 --> 00:04:09.120
David is putting on to see his true intentions.

00:04:09.120 --> 00:04:12.930
Him talking to Ellie while she was in the
cage is a great example of this.

00:04:12.930 --> 00:04:18.079
The entire time he’s being pretty vague
and is clearly lying to Ellie to win her trust.

00:04:18.079 --> 00:04:21.729
Then another one of my favorite scenes was
the dinner scene where they were making the

00:04:21.729 --> 00:04:24.670
stew and they brought in the quote on quote
deer meat.

00:04:24.670 --> 00:04:28.520
At this point, we assume this is actually
deer meat because the scene prior showed us

00:04:28.520 --> 00:04:31.669
David and video game Joel bringing back the
deer.

00:04:31.669 --> 00:04:35.270
Only to reveal that they walk in with the
deer as they are serving the food.

00:04:35.270 --> 00:04:41.100
It cleverly hits you with the oh shit, that
is not deer meat and it can only be something

00:04:41.100 --> 00:04:42.170
else.

00:04:42.170 --> 00:04:47.200
Just a very smart way to communicate to the
audience what the meat is without being obvious.

00:04:47.200 --> 00:04:50.949
They do clearly confirm that later on, but
that was more because they wanted Ellie to

00:04:50.949 --> 00:04:52.240
find out that they were cannibals.

00:04:52.240 --> 00:04:53.240
So that’s fine.

00:04:53.240 --> 00:04:56.680
Then also the small touch where David has
a much bigger plate of food in comparison

00:04:56.680 --> 00:04:59.759
to everyone else portrays how much he exploits
his power.

00:04:59.759 --> 00:05:02.830
You guys get to ration while I don’t.

00:05:02.830 --> 00:05:06.460
These details within the script that you can
understand without them blatantly telling

00:05:06.460 --> 00:05:09.740
you are hallmarks of what makes a script so
great.

00:05:09.740 --> 00:05:14.330
Looking at Scott Shephard who plays David,
I think he did a good job at being a more

00:05:14.330 --> 00:05:17.199
chill, warm, and inviting person to begin
with.

00:05:17.199 --> 00:05:20.949
It makes his character so unsettling because
we know that he’s the villain due to his

00:05:20.949 --> 00:05:25.229
juxtaposition to our main characters, and
we desperately try to figure out what is wrong

00:05:25.229 --> 00:05:26.229
with him.

00:05:26.229 --> 00:05:30.990
He’s good at hiding his intentions which
in of itself is also really terrifying.

00:05:30.990 --> 00:05:35.669
For a one-episode villain, this a masterful
example in writing and acting as to making

00:05:35.669 --> 00:05:38.520
a compelling antagonist in a short amount
of time.

00:05:38.520 --> 00:05:40.630
Just very impressive overall.

00:05:40.630 --> 00:05:43.870
Going into one complaint that I’ve been
seeing people say a lot that will probably

00:05:43.870 --> 00:05:48.470
boil over here, is the overall lack of infected
in comparison to the game.

00:05:48.470 --> 00:05:52.530
Now, this doesn’t bother me because the
show takes a realistic angle in how it tells

00:05:52.530 --> 00:05:53.650
its story.

00:05:53.650 --> 00:05:57.560
Joel tells us early on that there are basically
no infected out in the middle of nowhere in

00:05:57.560 --> 00:06:01.960
these forest regions because logically, these
are very rural areas.

00:06:01.960 --> 00:06:05.820
If people barely lived out here before the
outbreak then why would it make sense for

00:06:05.820 --> 00:06:07.789
there to be a ton of infected here.

00:06:07.789 --> 00:06:11.720
Along with being able to survive in these
harsh cold terrains that even humans have

00:06:11.720 --> 00:06:13.080
to take shelter in.

00:06:13.080 --> 00:06:16.960
This isn’t like the Walking Dead where there
are randomly hordes of zombies in the forest

00:06:16.960 --> 00:06:18.370
at all times.

00:06:18.370 --> 00:06:23.160
In that show, it’s an obvious contrivance
that fans naturally accept so they can see

00:06:23.160 --> 00:06:24.580
more zombies.

00:06:24.580 --> 00:06:29.930
However, for The Last of Us, it’s more grounded
in how they treat the mechanics of the world.

00:06:29.930 --> 00:06:34.370
Neil and Craig want to make an immersive and
realistic post-apocalyptic show.

00:06:34.370 --> 00:06:36.340
Not a flat-out zombie show.

00:06:36.340 --> 00:06:39.840
This includes having threats that are both
infected and human.

00:06:39.840 --> 00:06:43.740
So it makes the most sense to have infected
as a threat when they are in cities and when

00:06:43.740 --> 00:06:47.030
they aren’t, it makes more sense to use
humans.

00:06:47.030 --> 00:06:51.300
Because I know zombie fanatics will hate to
hear this, but the human characters and villains

00:06:51.300 --> 00:06:53.229
have more depth than zombies.

00:06:53.229 --> 00:06:54.960
That’s just how it is.

00:06:54.960 --> 00:06:59.150
It’s inherently more engaging to follow
the humans and although you can create some

00:06:59.150 --> 00:07:03.650
outstanding zombie sequences with rich tension
and action, there’s a lot more variety in

00:07:03.650 --> 00:07:05.360
terms of what humans can offer.

00:07:05.360 --> 00:07:10.629
And a reason why those zombie sequences like
in episode 5 hit so well, is because they

00:07:10.629 --> 00:07:11.870
are used in moderation.

00:07:11.870 --> 00:07:14.210
Also, it’s not like we’re deprived of
zombies.

00:07:14.210 --> 00:07:18.550
They’ve shown up in half of the episodes
so far and some of the coolest sequences included

00:07:18.550 --> 00:07:19.550
them.

00:07:19.550 --> 00:07:23.540
Using them more strategically in the narrative
increases their threat and lethality and overall

00:07:23.540 --> 00:07:25.930
makes them more menacing when they do show
up.

00:07:25.930 --> 00:07:30.259
I also keep seeing people say that they hate
how a character always dies when an infected

00:07:30.259 --> 00:07:35.400
is around and I’d argue that’s a good
thing because they retain their danger factor.

00:07:35.400 --> 00:07:39.540
I want to be scared and tensed up when the
infected are on screen and for it to not be

00:07:39.540 --> 00:07:41.870
like a shooting gallery like in the Walking
Dead.

00:07:41.870 --> 00:07:46.720
A couple of other episode-specific complaints
I’ve seen were the fact that Ellie was let

00:07:46.720 --> 00:07:51.849
go so easily in the beginning and the ending
feeling weird because the town felt non-existent.

00:07:51.849 --> 00:07:55.960
For the first one, David’s intentions were
pretty clear and he wanted to track Ellie

00:07:55.960 --> 00:07:57.569
back to where they were hiding.

00:07:57.569 --> 00:08:01.190
On top of him probably forming an attraction
to Ellie in this time.

00:08:01.190 --> 00:08:02.810
So that makes complete sense.

00:08:02.810 --> 00:08:06.650
For the ending, it doesn’t bother me that
much that the whole town wasn’t present.

00:08:06.650 --> 00:08:11.020
I feel like focusing primarily on David was
a lot better than having Joel kill dozens

00:08:11.020 --> 00:08:12.020
of people.

00:08:12.020 --> 00:08:16.100
Along with him being in a pretty rough state,
so it would be equally illogical for him to

00:08:16.100 --> 00:08:17.870
take on that many people.

00:08:17.870 --> 00:08:21.979
But I can buy the fact that the town wasn’t
alerted since Joel essentially wasn’t around

00:08:21.979 --> 00:08:23.190
for that long.

00:08:23.190 --> 00:08:26.810
On top of the building Ellie was in was not
showing much smoke externally.

00:08:26.810 --> 00:08:31.270
Also, the town’s overall strength is already
dwindling and it seemed like David took his

00:08:31.270 --> 00:08:34.940
strongest people with their gun supply to
fight Joel and lost.

00:08:34.940 --> 00:08:38.919
So even though the ending in this regard is
changed, I don’t think it hurts the overall

00:08:38.919 --> 00:08:40.539
quality of the episode.

00:08:40.539 --> 00:08:44.180
You don’t really need to have Joel fight
through all these people because the dramatic

00:08:44.180 --> 00:08:47.100
emphasis is primarily put on Ellie’s shoulders.

00:08:47.100 --> 00:08:49.760
Which she obviously delivers in spades.

00:08:49.760 --> 00:08:54.190
This section of the story is about Ellie’s
independence and ability to take care of herself

00:08:54.190 --> 00:08:56.190
against insurmountable odds.

00:08:56.190 --> 00:09:00.570
That’s why it resonates so well because
the underdog conflict within this episode

00:09:00.570 --> 00:09:02.070
is so strong.

00:09:02.070 --> 00:09:05.820
Ellie can’t rely on Joel so it makes the
obstacles she has to overcome that much more

00:09:05.820 --> 00:09:06.930
palpable.

00:09:06.930 --> 00:09:11.200
And even Joel having to take on the raiders
by himself was intense given how bad of a

00:09:11.200 --> 00:09:12.680
condition he was in.

00:09:12.680 --> 00:09:17.970
Craig Mazin and Neil Druckman consistently
deliver scenes with so much suspense and weight

00:09:17.970 --> 00:09:20.290
that the show always feels enjoyable.

00:09:20.290 --> 00:09:24.600
The grounded and realistic nature of the show
also helps this because the writers don’t

00:09:24.600 --> 00:09:27.720
introduce any bullshit into the script to
cheat the conflict.

00:09:27.720 --> 00:09:32.550
It’s just so refreshing having this show
and even their attention to detail and authenticity

00:09:32.550 --> 00:09:35.480
of the environment elevates the script.

00:09:35.480 --> 00:09:38.930
Like these actors were getting rolled by the
cold in this episode.

00:09:38.930 --> 00:09:42.720
Poor Ellie face planting into the snow was
just oof feelsbadman.

00:09:42.720 --> 00:09:47.550
I’m really excited going into the final
episode because if these past episodes are

00:09:47.550 --> 00:09:51.700
this great then just wait and see what the
finale has in store for you.

00:09:51.700 --> 00:09:54.360
Because it is going to be absolutely wild.

00:09:54.360 --> 00:09:58.450
Thank you for watching and let me know what
you thought about episode 8 of The Last of

00:09:58.450 --> 00:09:59.060
Us.

